//! Load switches: a part that passes its input rail to its output rail when its enable turns it
//! on (issue 1269).

import std::parts::transistor::PMosfet
import std::sources::{Rail, Reverse}

/// A load switch IC: a switch between two rails that shares their ground, on when its enable is
/// high. A concrete IC is a `part X provides LoadSwitch` that writes its pin table, against the
/// pins of this generic (`.in.pos`, `.in.neg`, `.out.pos`, `.enable`), and its datasheet's numbers
/// as narrowings (`.on_resistance &= 0mohm..60mohm`, `.max_current &= 2A`).
///
/// What it is for the checks (the owner, issue 1269). It is a converter between two rails, as a
/// regulator is: its output supplies what the loads on it draw, and its input draws that and its
/// quiescent current, so the current budget and the power tree see the output's loads on the
/// input's rail. The output's voltage is the input's less the drop across the switch, its on
/// resistance times the load. The DC analysis puts the on resistance between the two rails' `pos`
/// nets (`dc load_switch`), so the voltage of the output and the current of the input are the
/// circuit's, and the stage in front of the input is asked for the output's load.
///
/// The output is supplied when the enable can turn the switch on: a host's pin that drives the
/// enable, the enable strapped to a supply (the input's own, for a switch that is always on), or
/// a pull-up or any other passive part on its net. The switch is then taken as on, the worst case
/// for what the input supplies. An enable that is on a net with nothing else, that the board
/// states `unused`, or that only grounds, inputs and open-drain outputs are on, cannot turn it
/// on: the DC analysis leaves the switch open and the power tree says that its output has no
/// source, and why. An enable that a pull-up inside the IC holds high when it floats is taken as
/// off (the pin table says nothing of the pull-up): tie it to the input. An IC whose enable is
/// active low is not this generic.
///
/// Not checked: the switch's own heat (the drop times the load), its turn-on time and inrush
/// current, and an output that a switch which is off pulls down (a quick output discharge). An
/// IC sold as several order codes, with and without such a feature, lists them in a `.variants`
/// table, and a board that names the feature in `.needs` gets a code that has it (issue 1402). Its
/// reverse behaviour is data, `.reverse` (issue #1258): a board's `scenario` reads it, through
/// the on resistance while the switch is on and the body diode while it is off, unless it
/// blocks.
#[kind(ic)]
generic LoadSwitch {
    .designator &= "U"

    .in: Rail
    .out: Rail
    .in.neg ~ .out.neg

    pins {
        .in.pos  power
        .in.neg  power
        .out.pos power
        // High turns the switch on.
        .enable  input
    }

    /// The resistance of the switch while it is on, at its largest over the conditions the
    /// design runs in: the datasheet's maximum at the input voltage of the board.
    .on_resistance: Resistance
    /// The largest continuous current the switch passes.
    .max_current: Current
    /// The current the switch draws from its input for itself, as a range, as
    /// `LinearRegulator`'s is: the datasheet's quiescent current while it is on. A part that
    /// states nothing has the default, none.
    .quiescent_current: Current
    .quiescent_current ?= 0A
    /// What the switch does with current from its output back to its input (issue #1258). The
    /// default is a body diode (`std::sources::Reverse`); an IC with reverse-current blocking
    /// states `.reverse.path &= ReversePath::Blocking`.
    .reverse: Reverse
    /// What the design needs of the IC's order code (`.sw.needs &= QuickDischarge`, issue
    /// 1402): a feature of a provider's `.variants` table. A provider with no table has none.
    .needs: String optional

    // What the input draws is what the output supplies and the quiescent current, and the
    // output is the input less the drop across the switch.
    .in.current == -.out.current + .quiescent_current
    .out.voltage == .in.voltage - .on_resistance * (-.out.current)
    -.out.current <= .max_current
    // What a load draws at an instant is checked against the rated current (issue #750).
    .out.max_peak_current == .max_current

    dc load_switch .in .out .enable .on_resistance .quiescent_current
}

/// A P-channel MOSFET used as a load switch: its source on the input, its drain on the output and
/// its gate the enable, low to turn it on (issue 1269). The solver picks the MOSFET (`PMosfet`,
/// its catalog `p_mosfet`); a board narrows it from outside (`.sw.q.max_drain_current >= 2A`).
///
/// The MOSFET is a `~>` chain from the input's `pos` to the output's `pos`, so its `.current` is
/// what the output's loads draw and the input draws the same: the current budget and the power
/// tree see the output's loads on the input's rail, as for `LoadSwitch`. The output's voltage is
/// the input's less the drop across the channel. The DC analysis models the MOSFET as a switch
/// when what sets its gate is known (a host's pin, a resistor, a held net): on when the gate can
/// be driven past its threshold below the input, open when it cannot (see "DC analysis" in the
/// guide).
///
/// What it assumes: the enable is pulled to the input's ground to turn it on, so the gate-source
/// voltage while on is the input's voltage, and the channel blocks the input's voltage while it
/// is off. A board puts the pull-up that holds the gate at the input while the host lets go (a
/// resistor from `.enable` to the input's `pos`), and a level shifter where the host's high is
/// below the input.
module PmosLoadSwitch {
    .in: Rail
    .out: Rail
    .in.neg ~ .out.neg
    /// The gate: low turns the switch on.
    .enable: Pin
    .q: PMosfet
    /// The resistance of the channel while it is on: the MOSFET's.
    .on_resistance: Resistance
    .on_resistance == .q.on_resistance
    /// The largest continuous current the switch passes: the MOSFET's.
    .max_current: Current
    .max_current == .q.max_drain_current

    .in.pos ~> .q ~> .out.pos
    .enable ~ .q.gate

    .out.voltage == .in.voltage - .on_resistance * .q.current
    .q.gate_source_voltage == .in.voltage
    .q.drain_source_voltage == .in.voltage
}
